  Product key:  CU-TPDEFER-030
  Release:      April 28, 2026 (v1.8)

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Purpose:
    Defers specified third-party scripts past window.load. Catches
    app-injected <script src> tags at head-parse time, strips src,
    marks the element inert. On window.load (or 5s fallback) creates
    fresh <script> elements with original src and preserved data-*
    attributes, appended in capture order with async=false so execution
    order matches parser semantics.

  Target scope:
    Substring match against script URL. Configured patterns defer any
    matching script. Current pattern list:
      - 'judgeme'  — Judge.me review widget loader and dependencies.
                     Captured Apr 24 2026, ~645ms CPU on Moto G4 in LCP
                     window.

    DO NOT add 'klaviyo' to this list. Klaviyo is already deferred by
    chaos_unleashed_performance_deferrer.liquid (Performance Deferrer
    v2.5) which uses a script-creation override mechanism that catches
    parser-discovered scripts (this MutationObserver-based deferrer
    cannot — see "Why MutationObserver fails for parser-discovered
    scripts" below). Adding Klaviyo here creates two deferrers racing
    for the same script, which is a future maintenance bug.

  Why MutationObserver fails for parser-discovered scripts:
    The HTML preload scanner is a separate, faster pipeline than the
    main parser. It reads ahead in the buffer and dispatches network
    requests for <script src>, <img src>, <link href> etc. BEFORE the
    main parser executes inline IIFE scripts that would attach a
    MutationObserver. By the time the observer is attached, parser-
    discovered scripts have already been seen and queued by the
    preload scanner. Even if we strip src after the observer fires,
    the network request is already in flight, and execution will
    proceed when the response arrives.

    This deferrer DOES catch dynamically-injected scripts that fire
    AFTER our observer attaches — e.g., Judge.me's loader.js
    appending its widget script. For static <script src> tags emitted
    via content_for_header, use chaos_unleashed_performance_deferrer
    .liquid (which overrides Element.prototype.setAttribute and
    HTMLScriptElement.prototype.src) or chaos_unleashed_cfh_defer
    .liquid (which performs server-side string rewrite).

  Layout-shift reservation:
    On product templates only, emits an inline <style> block reserving
    min-width: 50% and min-height: 400px on the .jdgm-horizon-widget
    container that holds the main reviews carousel — but ONLY that
    container, not the inline preview-badge or per-card carousel
    widgets that share the same outer class. Selector uses :has() to
    require a .jdgm-review-widget descendant, which is unique to the
    reviews-section widget. Desktop-only via @media (min-width: 990px)
    because mobile layout stacks vertically.

  Safety:
    - Substring pattern match only; misses fail open (script executes normally)
    - MutationObserver self-terminates at window.load
    - 5s fallback timer catches broken load events
    - data-* attributes preserved through capture+restore for widgets
      that read their own config from the script element (e.g. Judge.me
      loader reads config from data attrs during init)
    - async=false on restored scripts preserves parser execution order
    - Capture queue self-limits; capacity-reached scripts fail open

  Debug:
    Set DEBUG = true during development. Runtime diagnostic exposed on
    window.__chaosTpDefer.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  v1.8 CHANGELOG (April 28, 2026):
    REVERT v1.7. Removed 'klaviyo' from Handled_Script_Patterns array
    (back to ['judgeme']). v1.7 added 'klaviyo' on the theory that
    deferring Klaviyo's bootstrap would reduce LCP. Investigation
    found two issues:
      1. Klaviyo is ALREADY deferred by chaos_unleashed_performance_
         deferrer.liquid (Performance Deferrer v2.5), which has
         'klaviyo' in its Handlers_Deferred_Scripts array and uses a
         script-creation interceptor (overriding Element.prototype
         .setAttribute + HTMLScriptElement.prototype.src) that DOES
         catch parser-discovered scripts. Adding Klaviyo here was
         redundant.
      2. This deferrer's MutationObserver mechanism cannot catch
         parser-discovered scripts (the preload scanner sees src
         before the observer attaches). Console verification with
         deferredScriptCount() confirmed only 1 script (Judge.me)
         was being captured even with 'klaviyo' in the patterns
         array — Klaviyo was slipping past the observer entirely.

    Net: the v1.7 'klaviyo' addition did nothing functional but
    created a maintenance trap of two deferrers competing. Reverting
    eliminates the trap. No LCP impact from this revert (Klaviyo was
    not actually being caught here anyway).

    DO NOT re-add Klaviyo to this list. See header comment.

  v1.7 CHANGELOG (April 28, 2026) — REVERTED in v1.8:
    Added 'klaviyo' to Handled_Script_Patterns array. Did not work
    (see v1.8 changelog for full investigation).

  v1.6 CHANGELOG (April 24, 2026):
